 "pd": {
  "basis": "traditional",
  "detail": "Anonymous American ballad in 19th-century oral circulation, no identifiable author. Clarence Ashley recorded it in 1933 and reported learning it from his grandfather in the 1800s; Alan Lomax collected Georgia Turner's version in Kentucky in 1937.",
  "sourceEdition": "Common folk transcription of the traditional melody, cross-checked against the tune family documented in the Lomax field collections of the 1930s ('The Rising Sun Blues').",
  "notes": "This encodes the TRADITIONAL tune with a plain functional-minor harmonization (Am, Dm, E, with diatonic C and F) -- NOT the 1964 rock arrangement, whose arpeggiated Am-C-D-F treatment and recording remain in copyright; the underlying ballad is traditional. Encoded in its real 6/8 on the eighth-note pulse: 6 beats per bar, dotted quarter = d 3; tempoBPM is the eighth pulse (174 ~= dotted quarter 58). Natural-minor degrees; the melody's natural 7 (G) is deg 7 acc 0, the cadential raised 7th (G#) is deg 7 acc 1 oct -1 at the E-major cadences. The 'There is a' pickup lives at the end of the intro and again at the end of the verse; internal line pickups sit at the ends of their preceding bars. Melody and changes only; no lyrics. Confidence medium: the tune circulates in phrase-level variants; this follows the widely sung traditional contour."
 },
